00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So remember last class we did expected utility theory, or we started with expected utility theory.

56
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:12,000
And the whole idea of expected utility theory is that you have to have some utility wealth function, such as u is equal to the square root of your wealth.

57
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
And then the idea of expected utility theory is that everybody maximizes a linearly...

58
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Maximizes the utility weighted by probabilities of different events occurring.

59
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
So, and the key thing is that the weights are always linear.

60
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So if something has a 30% chance of happening, you put.3 ahead of whatever the event is, and then you plug it in.

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So, I think the example I gave last time was in fact just exactly this.

62
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
So u is equal to the square root of w, where w is.5.

63
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:59,000
And then if you were evaluating two lottery tickets, one with a 50% chance of 10,000 and a 50% chance of 0.

64
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:07,000
That's one lottery ticket, and the other lottery ticket is 2,000 with probability 100%.

65
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
So these are the two lottery tickets. Which one do you want?

66
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:22,000
The way, what expected utility theory says that you will do is for this one you take.5 times the square root of 10,000 plus.5 times the square root of 0.

67
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And then compare that to 1 times the square root of 2,000 and whichever one is higher, so the expected utility theory says you will do whichever one is higher.

68
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Alright, so and if you plug it through, that one comes out to 50, that one comes out to 44.72.

69
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
So that is the preferred lottery ticket for someone with that expected utility function.

70
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Alright, now just one math aside if you're curious, if you have never heard of any of this don't worry about it.

71
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Maybe just a small, small snap.

72
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:04,000
So you might remember when you were doing utility theory, did you ever hear that utility functions are invariant up to a monotonic transformation?

73
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
So that means if you maximize utility you can also square the utility function, you can add 10 to it.

74
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Anything that keeps the utilities that were higher before higher afterwards, anything that preserves the relative ranking is a perfectly fine thing to do.

75
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Alright, so that is, we're reversing it.

76
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
It would not be okay to say take the cosine of your utility function because cosine is not another monotonic function.

77
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Right, cosine goes like that, okay.

78
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Alright, now expected utility theory, expected utility functions are not like that.

79
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:44,000
So you can go and do a monotonic transformation of expected utility, which would expect utility function and get a different answer.

80
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
So all that you're allowed to do with expected utility function is what's called an affine transformation.

81
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
That means you can multiply expected utility by any constant or you can add a constant to it.

82
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
And then it's still unique.

83
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,000
So expected utility theory of radical W is the same as like 2 radical W minus 8.

84
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
That would also be fine, would also give you exactly the same answer every time.

85
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
If you wanted to square it, that would actually change the answer.

86
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
So you could not do that with an expected utility function.

87
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Now when you first hear about expected, oh yeah, Ash.

88
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Shouldn't be any positive affine transformation.

89
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Let's see, ah yes, that's true, yes.

90
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
So yeah, it's true if you multiply it by negative something, yes, you're absolutely right.

91
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
So yes, yes, so that is correct.

92
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
So yeah, any positive function, although you can add or subtract any constant.

93
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
That's very good.

94
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Alright, now at first glance expected utility theory seems like it's just a tautology and like what else would somebody do?

95
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
But expected utility theory actually has some real implications, which can be tested.

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
And a number of them have shown to be wrong, at least under certain conditions,

97
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
although still may be a theory that is generally useful.

98
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Alright, so here is one of the implications of expected utility theory.

99
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Now this one may seem totally obvious to you, but it's not obvious to normal people.

100
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
So keep that in mind.

101
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Alright, so suppose that you're in different, so one implication is the compounding result.

102
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,000
It says that consumers are indifferent between a 50% chance of a 50% chance and a 25% chance.

103
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Well of course, who wouldn't be?

104
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Lots of people actually.

105
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:22,000
There are a lot of people who, if they could either go and roll a four-sided die and they went on a four,

106
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
or they flip a coin and see what happens, and then they flip another coin,

107
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,000
if that coin comes out right, they'll say, oh I'll do the second one, that one's safer.

108
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
And you're like, what? How is it safer?

109
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Well, there's a feeling of, look, I just do a 50% risk at first, and then I see whether I'm okay.

110
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
It's like you jump to a ledge of a mountain, and then you get there, okay, I'm okay.

111
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
Now I'll try to jump to the higher ledge.

112
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Okay, and now I made it.

113
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Whereas jumping, just doing one big leap would freak them out.

114
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:58,000
So, and again, if this seems really weird to you, these are very normal reactions.

115
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
And let's see, I'm trying to remember, like when I was on vacation, I heard someone say something exactly like this,

116
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
and I just sat there nodding.

117
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Yes, of course, of course, right, you would want to go and resolve the uncertainty sequentially.

118
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,000
That's very important, because otherwise it would be very scary.

119
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
All right, so I'm trying to remember, what was it?

120
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Maybe it will come to me, but.

121
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
So you may have seen this with, like kids are often like this, where they don't want to just have the dice get rolled.

122
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Let's just roll one die and see how that goes.

123
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
And then we'll roll the next die and see how that goes.

124
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Right, I mean, so just think about someone, they have to roll 10 dice, and they have to get a good overall roll.

125
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Imagine someone wants to roll the dice one at a time, because somehow that matters to them.

126
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:46,000
So if that matters to you, then you do not actually have expected utility theory.

127
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Expected utility theory does not describe you.

128
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
All right, now another thing, so linear probabilities.

129
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:58,000
So in expected utility theory, you won't see a big nonlinear difference in the utility

130
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:04,000
between having a 0% chance of a risk and a 0.1% chance of a risk.

131
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:10,000
All right, now, in the real world, a lot of people treat this as a big difference.

132
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
But if this works, there's no chance it could go wrong.

133
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Yeah, but there's only like a one in a billion chance anyway, so what's the difference?

134
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
And you may say, well, I mean, that could still be consistent with expected utility theory.

135
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Here's the key thing.

136
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,000
If people regard there as being a big difference between 0 and one in a billion,

137
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
but a small difference between one in a billion and two in a billion,

138
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
that's where we've seen the violation of expected utility theory.

139
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
So if going from a small amount to zero matters a lot to you,

140
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,000
and going from a small amount to twice a small amount, a probability barely matters at all to you,

141
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
you violated the theory.

142
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:52,000
All right, now, you may have noticed, for example, that people will try to sell you products

143
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:58,000
by saying this gives you certainty, like for an extended warranty.

144
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Like you'll say, look, probably you're going to be fine,

145
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
but you can be perfectly safe if I sell you this warranty.

146
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Now, there's something very fishy about claims like this because is it really true

147
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
that you can ever be perfectly safe with anything?

148
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,000
No.

149
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,000
I mean, what if you leave town and then the store that gives the warranty,

150
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
it's too inconvenient for you to go and get your money back?

151
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Well, then you're not perfectly safe.

152
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
What if the product kills you and so you're not alive to collect the money?

153
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
That's not perfectly safe.

154
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,000
But anytime someone is trying to go and make a big deal out of absolute certainty,

155
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
there's the one problem that they can't really sell you absolute certainty.

156
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
They're actually doing something like impounding.

157
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:46,000
They're saying, well, if you're alive and you still live in town and you don't lose your receipt

158
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:51,000
and 77 other conditions, then it's perfectly certain that you'll get your money back

159
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and we haven't gone bankrupt and all these other things.

160
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:00,000
But again, that's really just like not only is this a nonlinearity,

161
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:08,000
but there's also like how is that so great because I still actually don't have anything like certainty.

162
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
But the idea is, well, but if all these things happen, then it would be certain.

163
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,000
And that's really valuable for something to be absolutely certain if 77 unlikely events occur first.

164
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
How is that particularly good?

165
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:22,000
I don't understand.

166
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
And if that's what you're talking, then, well, you're not really an expending warranty buyer anyway.

167
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
So just move along.

168
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:30,000
All right.

169
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Right now, expected utility theory is not saying you're going to value $1,000 a hundred times as much as $10.

170
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
It's only the probabilities that matter linearly, right?

171
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Again, which is one of the harder distinctions to grasp.

172
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So it's the probabilities that matter linearly.

173
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:56,000
So it just says that no matter how bad an event is, going from 0% to 1% is the same as going from 1% to 2%,

174
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
which again seems to be empirically wrong for a lot of people.

175
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
There's a lot of value people put on certainty.

176
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:09,000
I gave a talk on immigration once, and then a guy in the audience asked me, well, if you're wrong, do I get my country back?

177
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:16,000
And I was thinking of, if you know the movie Fargo, where William H. Macy keeps saying, I guarantee that I will pay you back.

178
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
There's your guarantee.

179
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
I guarantee it.

180
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
All right.

181
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
So I was tempted to say, yes, I guarantee you can have your country back.

182
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
Just do what I say, and then I'll give you your country back.

183
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
But I want to be honest and say, well, of course not.

184
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
How could I give you – I can't honestly promise to give you your country back.

185
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Just like how can you guarantee me that if computer development occurs, the Terminator scenario won't occur?

186
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
I don't have any guarantees.

187
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,000
There's a think tank at Oxford that worries about this all the time.

188
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:47,000
All I can say is, seems really unlikely, but that's the best I can do.

189
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,000
I can't really guarantee it.

190
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:52,000
But the good news is, you shouldn't really – it's not that important to get a guarantee.

191
00:12:52,000 --> 00:13:01,000
If you've got an expect utility function, then a guarantee is not that much better than having just a marginal risk reduction.

192
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
All right.

193
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:04,000
Okay.

194
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
All right.

195
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Now, again, of course, the more you think about it, the weirder it is, because psychologically, these things seem to matter a lot to people.

196
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And, again, so think about where you're really nervous about a standardized test score.

197
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:23,000
You go and log in, and then, fine, the score is okay, and then your stress goes down from a lot to zero.

198
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:29,000
Now, that's a little odd because, wait a second, how do you know that you actually logged into the right web page?

199
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
And how do you know the results are reported correctly?

200
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
And, you know, it could be like the best picture in the Academy Award, and they announced the wrong winner.

201
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:44,000
So, really, like, it doesn't make sense for your stress to go down from a lot to zero just because you get this information.

202
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Maybe to fall a lot, but as long as there's still some uncertainty, seems like there should still be some concern.

203
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:54,000
All right.

204
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
So any other questions about expect utility theory?

205
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:03,000
This is a basic building block in any choice under uncertainty, any paper that you see that analyzes choice under uncertainty.

206
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:10,000
You almost always use expect utility theory, and, of course, there's a whole experimental literature testing expect utility theory.

207
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Normally, you do experiments when you're looking for an error in it, looking for an anomaly.

208
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
It doesn't mean the theory is totally wrong or useless.

209
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:18,000
Yes?

210
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Can you speak a little bit about whether expect utility theory makes or no values cardinal or effective?

211
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Sure.

212
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:34,000
So the answer is expect utility theory is still just saying it's summarizing what you'll do in every logically possible situation.

213
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:45,000
So in that sense, you could say that it is still ordinal, but it does contain extra information that a regular utility function doesn't have.

214
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Because regular utility function can only say which of different bundles will you choose.

215
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
It can't say which of different gambles will you choose.

216
00:14:52,000 --> 00:15:00,000
And by adding in this additional information, it actually can summarize what you would do for every conceivable gamble.

217
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,000
So there's more information, but it still is technically ordinal.

218
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:05,000
All right.

